  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1143 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nA pleasant day is underway with temperatures in the 70s under\npartly cloudy skies. Temperatures will warm a few degrees warmer\nthan the last few days as an upper level ridge expands across the\narea. The hottest temperatures will be felt in our southwest\nzones, around 90 degrees, with upper 70s and 80s elsewhere. The\nridge will not be able to amplify over the Mid-South due to a\ndeep trough off the coast of the Carolinas. Temperatures will\nremain slightly above normal for the end of September generally\nin the mid to upper 80s. Areas in closest proximity to the\ntrough, have the best chance at remaining near normal.\n\nDue to the region being sandwiched between the ridge and the low,\nbenign weather conditions will persist through the weekend.\nPatchy to locally dense fog could occur however each night as\ndewpoint depressions sink under light winds and mostly clear\nskies. The next large scale pattern change to affect the region\nwill be the remnants of what is currently Hurricane Polo. Polo is\nforecast to approach the Baja California Peninsula early next\nweek. Guidance is not resolving this system's evolution well in\nthe long term. The most likely scenario amongst current guidance\nis Polo absorbing into a cut-off low with an additional system\nand stretching a large cold front across much of the southern US.\nThis would be our first chance at widespread rainfall on Tuesday\nnight into Wednesday. Current severe weather probabilities from\nthe LREF are less than 20%, but this will need to be monitored as\nthe system works its way onshore.\n\nDNM",
